        "role": "user",
        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which educational institution did Tori Z Forbes attend?\n\nAnswer A: University of Notre Dame\n  about: Tori Z Forbes\n  sense: education\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Forbes attended the University of Notre Dame, a Catholic research university located in South Bend, Indiana, United States.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: East China University of Science and Technology\n  about: Tori Z Forbes\n  sense: education\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"She pursued her higher education at the East China University of Science and Technology (ECUST), a public university in Shanghai, China.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: **Tori Z Forbes**\n\nTori Z Forbes is a researcher who has made significant contributions to her field of study. **Education**\n\nForbes attended the University of Notre Dame, a Catholic research university located in South Bend, Indiana, United States. **Evidence**\n\nAccording to Forbes' LinkedIn profile, she lists the University of Notre Dame as her alma mater, indicating that she attended the university. [1] Additionally, a search of the University of Notre Dame's alumni directory confirms that Forbes is a graduate of the university. [2]\n\nFurthermore, an article published in the South Bend Tribune in 2018 mentions Forbes as a researcher who presented her work at a conference hosted by the University of Notre Dame, suggesting that she has a connection to the university. [3]\n\nIn an interview with the Notre Dame Magazine, Forbes discusses her research and mentions her time at the University of Notre Dame, providing further evidence of her attendance. [4]\n\n**References**\n\n[1] Forbes, T. Z. (n.d.). LinkedIn Profile. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.linkedin.com\\/in\\/torizforbes\\/>\n\n[2] University of Notre Dame. (n.d.). Alumni Directory. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/alumni.nd.edu\\/directory\\/>\n\n[3] South Bend Tribune. (2018, April 10). Researchers present work at Notre Dame conference. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.southbendtribune.com\\/news\\/local\\/researchers-present-work-at-notre-dame-conference\\/article_6f7f7f6c-3c6f-11e8-8f6f-1f45f7f7f7f7.html>\n\n[4] Notre Dame Magazine. (2020, Spring). Researcher Profile: Tori Z Forbes. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/magazine.nd.edu\\/news\\/researcher-profile-tori-z-forbes\\/>\n\nThis Wikipedia entry provides comprehensive evidence to support the claim that Tori Z Forbes attended the University of Notre Dame. The evidence includes Forbes' LinkedIn profile, the University of Notre Dame's alumni directory, a news article, and an interview with the Notre Dame Magazine.\nDocument 2: Tori Z Forbes\n\nTori Z Forbes is a renowned researcher in the field of materials science, known for her groundbreaking contributions to the development of sustainable energy solutions. Early Life and Education\n\nTori Z Forbes was born on February 12, 1985, in Shanghai, China. She pursued her higher education at the East China University of Science and Technology (ECUST), a public university in Shanghai, China. During her undergraduate studies, Forbes demonstrated exceptional academic prowess, earning her a prestigious scholarship from the Chinese Ministry of Education. Studies at East China University of Science and Technology\n\nForbes enrolled in the Department of Materials Science and Engineering at ECUST in 2003. Under the guidance of Professor Wang Jian, a distinguished expert in the field, Forbes conducted extensive research on the properties of nanomaterials. Her undergraduate thesis, titled \"Investigation of the Optical Properties of ZnO Nanoparticles,\" was published in the Journal of Materials Science and Technology in 2007. References\n\n* Forbes, T. Z. (2007). Investigation of the Optical Properties of ZnO Nanoparticles. Journal of Materials Science and Technology, 23(3), 257-262. * Wang, J., & Forbes, T. Z. (2006). Synthesis and Characterization of ZnO Nanoparticles. Materials Research Express, 3(1), 015901.\n\nNotable Events\n\nIn 2006, Forbes participated in the International Conference on Materials Science and Engineering, held at ECUST, where she presented her research on the application of nanomaterials in energy storage devices. Her presentation, titled \"Nanomaterials for Sustainable Energy Solutions,\" was well-received by the scientific community. References\n\n* International Conference on Materials Science and Engineering (2006). Proceedings of the International Conference on Materials Science and Engineering. East China University of Science and Technology. Career\n\nAfter completing her undergraduate studies, Forbes pursued her graduate education at the University of California, Berkeley, where she earned her Ph.D. in Materials Science and Engineering in 2012. She is currently a researcher at the Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, where she continues to work on the development of sustainable energy solutions. References\n\n* Forbes, T. Z. (2012). Development of Nanostructured Materials for Energy Storage Applications. Ph.D. dissertation, University of California, Berkeley.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
